    "narrative": "The focus for Ghost Ride the WHIP today centers on capitalizing on high-ceiling matchups against questionable starting pitching. **Tyler Soderstrom** leads the charge with a 76.91 score; his placement in the cleanup spot against the struggling Ryan Johnson (5.29 xERA) provides a massive situational boost, and the right-on-right dynamic platoon advantage further cements him as our primary offensive anchor. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** represents our best value play at 72.32. While he sits in the seven-hole, his elite sprint speed and a significant platoon edge against the left-handed Dakota Hudson\u2014coupled with a pitcher whose slow delivery is tailor-made for a stolen base attempt\u2014make him an absolute must-start despite the lower batting order. **Josh Naylor** also warrants a strong recommendation at 66.95, as he maintains a favorable split against Baz, providing the necessary floor to balance the volatility of our lower-order bats.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the card was the exclusion of **Travis Bazzana**. With a score of 34.68, he fell short of the 40-point efficiency floor required to warrant a start. While his leadoff position is usually a catalyst for R/SB production, the combination of a tough matchup against Drohan and a severe negative platoon edge (L/L) effectively killed his daily projection. Elsewhere, the lineup construction remains steady, though we continue to monitor the status of **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who remains sidelined on the IL. We are proceeding with **Cole Young** (48.79) and **Brice Turang** (48.84) as functional starters; while neither reaches the \"Strong\" 60-point threshold, they offer enough efficiency against Baz and Messick to avoid burning game caps on bench options like **Henry Bolte** (58.30), whose high park-factor score against Johnson keeps him on the periphery of our daily utility consideration.",
